@divider num="I–IV" kind="AFTER THE DOSSIERS" title="The Torah/and TKC" sub="Where the four books touch the world, by source, with every guess labeled" foot="FIVE PAGES"

@page rh="THE TORAH AND TKC · 1 OF 5 · GENESIS AND EXODUS IN THE WORLD" m="TKC: TR · TL · CR · DR3 · AM · MEN; FR 0; FR 33; FR 35; FR 39"
# Genesis and Exodus in the world
rec:
The phases | Phase One belongs to the Temple and is called Genesis, "Who are you?" Phase Two belongs to the Crown and is called Exodus, "How smart are you?" (TR). POSSIBLE: The Crown's phase is named for the book about a ruler who loses an argument to two midwives and a labor force.
Eden and the Eve problem | Eden is named for the garden (TL). The Broadcast's name for its narrative against a candidate whose intelligence becomes the story is the Eve problem, the woman "blamed for asking rather than thanked" (TR). INTERPRETATION: Genesis 3 doesn't curse the woman or blame her for asking. The blame in the chapter comes from the man (3.12), and the Broadcast's narrative runs his defense.
Genesis 3.16 in the Assessment | The Temple's marriage theology (CR) depends on reading 3.16 as prescription. POSSIBLE: One of the three questions the Prelate writes for Eden personally (TR, name changed under FR 33) asks her to explain 3.16, and the grade depends on which reading she gives.
Leah | Eden's younger sister Leah is a Commonwife under Temple review for not conceiving within the Covenant timeline (TL, MEN). In Genesis, Leah is the fertile wife, and the three barren matriarchs are the women the promise runs through. POSSIBLE: The Temple's review quotes "be fruitful and multiply" and never mentions Sarah, Rebekah or Rachel.

@page rh="THE TORAH AND TKC · 1 OF 5 · GENESIS AND EXODUS IN THE WORLD, CONTINUED" m="TKC: TR · TL · CR · DR3 · AM · MEN; FR 0; FR 33; FR 35; FR 39"
# Genesis and Exodus in the world, continued
rec:
Reassignment and Tamar | Miriam was reassigned to another man's household after Thomas left (FR 0). Thomas left voluntarily (FR 3). How reassignment works is deliberately open. POSSIBLE: The Temple grounds it in levirate logic (Gen 38, Deut 25.5–10), which in scripture protects a widow's claim, and the Empire uses it to keep an abandoned wife inside the quota.
Hagar and naming | Hagar is the one person in scripture who names God. Celeste gave Eden a second name that's used only in the house (TL). INTERPRETATION: Both are names the official record doesn't hold.
Succession | Crest's claim came through survivorship and not birth order, and Dorian is the rightful heir's son (FR 35, 39). Crest is Dorian's uncle (FR 35). POSSIBLE: The Temple preaches Jacob over Esau and Judah over Reuben to make the 2061 succession sound like election.
Shiphrah and Puah | POSSIBLE: The model for a Vowed officer who quietly doesn't comply. INTERPRETATION: Eden's Public Testimony, where every sentence is defensible and the whole is incendiary (TR), is the midwives' answer to Pharaoh at book length.
Exodus 20.12 in the Assessment | Draft Three turns "Honor thy father and thy mother" into a question about reporting an Unbound woman who housed her children without male authority (DR3). Celeste is that woman.

@page rh="THE TORAH AND TKC · 2 OF 5 · LEVITICUS AND DEUTERONOMY IN THE WORLD" m="TKC: TL · DOM · CR · DR3 · MEN; FR 32; FR 41–43"
# Leviticus and Deuteronomy in the world
rec:
Joseph's administration | POSSIBLE: Imperial schoolbooks teach Joseph as the model administrator and the Fracture Years as Egypt's famine, with the Coalition as the steward who stored grain. The Restoration Act made rights conditional (TL), and Genesis 47 is the scriptural picture of a people trading freedom for food and thanking the crown.
The curse of Ham | Aurelia was built over Atlanta's Black memory (DOM). OPEN QUESTION: Does the Temple teach the curse of Ham? If a character uses it, the narration has to know it's a documented misuse.
Passover and the household | INTERPRETATION: Celeste's kitchen table (TL), the Salt Ceremony and the old way of burial (DOM) are household religion of the kind Exodus 12 and Deuteronomy 6 describe, which is why the Temple can't license them.
Salt | Leviticus 2.13 requires "the salt of the covenant" on every grain offering. POSSIBLE: The women's Salt Ceremony (DOM) uses the Temple's own covenant salt, and nobody in the Temple has noticed.

@page rh="THE TORAH AND TKC · 2 OF 5 · LEVITICUS AND DEUTERONOMY IN THE WORLD, CONTINUED" m="TKC: TL · DOM · CR · DR3 · MEN; FR 32; FR 41–43"
# Leviticus and Deuteronomy in the world, continued
rec:
Inspection | POSSIBLE: The Vowed household assessments (MEN) and the Designation (DOM) borrow the Leviticus 13 procedure, with a moral weight Leviticus keeps out.
The scapegoat | POSSIBLE: The Broadcast's antagonist narrative, assigned to the candidate who threatens a comfortable outcome (TR), is the Empire's Azazel goat. The confession is spoken over her, and she's sent out.
Leviticus 25.10 | POSSIBLE: The Empire can't read the Liberty Bell verse aloud. In Pacific, where people remember trying to leave (DOM), somebody still can.
Deuteronomy 13 | POSSIBLE: The command to report a family member who secretly worships other gods is the Temple's warrant for the Vowed as surveillance (CR) and for the Assessment's reporting question (DR3).
Guilt by household | "Children shall not be put to death for their fathers" (Deut 24.16). The Empire classifies children by household registration (FR 32). INTERPRETATION: Deuteronomy, Ezekiel 18 and the Empire disagree about where guilt lives.
Runaways | POSSIBLE: Ungoverned crossings through Sable (DOM) are the case Deuteronomy 23.15–16 was written for, and the Temple never reads it in that Dominion.
Josiah's scroll and the ledger | Azrael found the ledger in the east wing archive, Dorian removed it with the Covenant of Dissent, and Judah has been looking for the Dissent since before his brother's exile (TL, FR 41–43). POSSIBLE: Judah is a Josiah who hasn't found his scroll.

@page rh="THE TORAH AND TKC · 3 OF 5 · WHAT THE TEMPLE CIRCULATES" m="TKC: DOM · SANCTUM; FR 33"
# What it teaches and what it shelves
POSSIBLE: The three "Dominion's" spreads from the dossiers, gathered in one place. Every row is a construction to test.
tabrl:
| Book | What the Temple circulates | What it leaves out of public teaching |
| Exodus | The sea, as a deliverance already accomplished in the founding | Chapter 1, where the resistance starts with two midwives who lied to a king |
| Exodus | The commandments from the second clause on | The preamble, which grounds the right to command in ending forced labor |
| Exodus | Moses the obedient lawgiver | Moses arguing God out of a decision twice |
| Leviticus | Holiness as personal purity, chapters 18 and 20 as household law | Chapter 25 entire, and the harvest edge, the same-day wage and the interest ban |
| Leviticus | Restitution as an offering paid to the Temple | The wronged person is paid first, plus a fifth |
| Deuteronomy | The curses of chapter 28 as the explanation for every disaster | The law of the king |
| Deuteronomy | Teaching as the Temple's office | Chapter 6, where parents teach at home with no priest present |
| Deuteronomy | The conquest chapters as precedent | The runaway-slave clause and the seventh-year release |
box: The pattern
INTERPRETATION: The pattern is the same in all three. The Empire keeps Deuteronomy's enforcement and drops its constitution, keeps Leviticus's machinery of exclusion and drops its machinery of release, and teaches the exodus as finished so only the obedience half applies now.
/end
corr: SUPERSEDED: The old companion's Exodus page marked "Prelate Huldah's office" as canon. FR 33 renamed the Prelate. The working name is Prelate Aurelia, POSSIBLE, and whether the office manages the Temple's teaching of Exodus is only POSSIBLE now.
prompt: The row I'd add, and the book it comes from | fill

